es unshown \u2014 safe to discard. I have one T4, no other T tiles except T3 and T9 which are already punged. No way to make a chow with T4 (would need T5/T6). Not contributing to any potential pattern like All Simples or Pure Straight.\n- **Play W6**: W6 has only 1 unshown, and I\u2019m discarding the last unseen W6? Wait \u2014 unshown W6: 1, and I hold W6 \u2192 if I play it, it\u2019s the last one. Risky? Possibly, but W6 is middle tile, not terminal/honor. No one has shown interest in W6. Across player has W4s, next has W7-8-9 \u2014 they might be going for upper Characters. Playing W6 could feed a possible W5-W6-W7 chow, but W5 is already exhausted (unshown: 0) \u2014 so W5 is gone. Thus, no one can use W6 in a chow requiring W5. Similarly, W7 is still available (3 unshown), but W6-W7-W8 would require W8 (0 unshown) \u2014 impossible. So W6 cannot complete any chow now. Safe to discard.\n- **Play J1**: I have a pair of J1s. Discarding one breaks the pair. J1 has 2 unshown \u2014 so two copies remain unseen. If I discard a J1, then only one remains, possibly allowing an opponent to claim it for a pung or kong. But Peng J1 is possible only if someone has two J1s. Given that no one has shown J1 melds, it's unlikely. Still, breaking a potential dragon pung (J1 = Red Dragon) might lose scoring opportunities.\n\nI have two pungs (T3, T9), one chow (B7-B8-B9), and a near-pung of J1. If I keep J1, I can wait for another J1 to complete a pung \u2014 worth 2 points (Dragon Pung). Also, with three pungs and a chow, aiming for **All Pungs** (6 points) is possible if I convert the chow into a pung or get another pung. But I need another pung.\n\nAlternatively, if I draw or get a J1, I can have two pungs of dragons (if I promote to kong later), supporting Two Dragon Pungs (6 points). Currently, only one dragon pair.\n\nBut immediate win? Not yet. Need to improve hand.\n\nBest improvement path: aim for **All Pungs** or **Two Dragon Pungs**, or even **Half Flush** (honors + one suit). But my melds span T, B, W \u2014 too diverse.\n\nWait \u2014 I have pungs in T3 and T9 \u2014 both Bamboo? No: T = Bamboo, W = Characters, B = Dots.\n\nT3 and T9 are both Bamboo suit \u2014 same suit. Could go for **Pure Shifted Pungs**? T3, T9 \u2014 gap too big. Or **Triple Pung**? Only two so far.\n\nNo third pung in same number.\n\nAlternative: **Three Kongs** (32 pts) \u2014 but I have none concealed, and only one kong opportunity via promotion, but no current kong.\n\nBack to discards:\n\n- **Play B7**: Removes a tile not helping progress. But B7 is part of a completed chow \u2014 redundant. No benefit in keeping.\n- **Play T4**: Useless tile \u2014 no connection to any sequence. Safest discard from usefulness.\n- **Play W6**: Cannot form chow for anyone (W5 and W8 gone). Low risk.\n- **Play J1**: Breaks potential pung. J1 is a dragon \u2014 valuable for scoring. Better to keep for now.\n\nSo best discards: T4 or B7.\n\nBut consider patterns:\n\n- **All Pungs** requires four pungs. I have two pungs and one chow. Need to upgrade chow to pung or add more pungs.\n- If I keep J1, I can try to get another J1 to form pung \u2014 concealed, which helps **Concealed Hand** or **Fully Concealed Hand** (4 pts).\n- T4 and W6 are dead-end simples \u2014 no value.\n\nBetween B7 and T4: B7 is part of a chow, but I already used it \u2014 having an extra B7 doesn\u2019t help unless I want a pair, but I don\u2019t have another B7 to pair with (I have one B7 in hand, one in meld \u2014 so singleton now). Wait: meld uses one B7, I hold one \u2192 total two. Unshown B7: 2 \u2192 so two others exist unseen. Possible to draw another? But risky.\n\nBut B7 could be used by others \u2014 previous player has [B6,B7,B8], so they already used B7 \u2014 likely don\u2019t need more. Across player has B4,B5,B6 \u2014 no B7. Next player no B. So probably safe.\n\nBut **T4** is completely disconnected \u2014 no sequence possible (no T5, T6 in hand), and T3 and T9 are isolated pungs. T4 is useless.\n\nThus, **T4** is the most worthless tile.\n\nAlso, unshown T4: 3 \u2014 means no one is hoarding it \u2014 safe to discard.\n\nReason: T4 is a simple tile with no connection to any existing or potential meld in my hand. It does not contribute to any high-scoring pattern, and discarding it poses minimal risk to opponents. In contrast, J1 offers potential for a Dragon Pung (2 points), and B7, while redundant, is less risky than breaking a potential dragon pair. W6 is also safe but slightly more connected to the Character suit where others are active.\n\nAnswer: Play T4"}
